    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A much smaller defending force of foreigners and citizens held about 20 km of walls against a much larger Ottoman army and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, and no relief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> arrived in time.</li><li>The sack was severe. Thousands were killed and, by one count, around 30,000 people were enslaved; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a> ordered the looting stopped and issued a proclamation to those who had hidden or fled.</li><li>Many modern historians treat the fall as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>, and the capture of the last Roman capital as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On 22 April <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a> had ships hauled on greased logs across the hill at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a>, bypassing the chain across its mouth that the defenders had counted on to keep Ottoman ships out.</p></aside>
    </section>

