    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1452</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> builds the fortress of Rumeli Hisari on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a> declares church union valid on 12 December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text">Giovanni Giustiniani arrives in January with about 700 men and is given command of the land walls</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text">The Ottoman army encamps outside the city on 2 April and the siege begins on 6 April</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a> has his ships dragged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> on 22 April, and the fire-ship attack of 28 April fails</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text">Ottoman mining from mid-May is met by counter-mines, and Constantine refuses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a>'s offer of terms on 21 May</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text">Shortly after midnight on 29 May the final assault begins, Giustiniani is wounded and the walls are overwhelmed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text">Looting and enslavement follow for part of three days, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a> halts them and has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> made a mosque</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1460</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>, held by Constantine's brothers, falls to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="fall-of-constantinople" data-v="dc73de39ff" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies take their positions</b> The Ottoman army camped outside the land walls. The European regulars lay along the walls, the troops from Anatolia south of the Lycus, and the guns and Janissaries near the Mesoteichion. Constantine held the Mesoteichion and Giustiniani the Charisian Gate to the north.</li><li><b>The guns open fire and the chain holds the fleet out</b> Mehmed's cannons fired on the walls for weeks, but the Byzantines repaired most of the damage after each shot. The Ottoman fleet made probing attacks but could not enter the Golden Horn because of the chain.</li><li><b>Ships are hauled over the hill into the Horn</b> Mehmed had a road of greased logs laid across Galata and dragged his ships over the hill into the Golden Horn, bypassing the chain. This threatened the supply ships from Pera and demoralised the defenders.</li><li><b>The fire-ship attack on the hauled ships fails</b> The defenders tried to destroy the Ottoman ships in the Golden Horn with fire ships. The Ottomans forced the Christians to retreat with many casualties.</li><li><b>The final assault begins at the Blachernae walls</b> The Christian troops of the Ottoman Empire attacked first, followed by irregular azaps and Anatolian Turkmen, who focused on a damaged section of the Blachernae walls. The Turkmen breached it and entered the city but were quickly pushed back.</li><li><b>The Janissaries attack and the Genoese give way</b> The last wave, the elite Janissaries, attacked the walls. Giustiniani was grievously wounded and his evacuation caused panic among the defenders. His Genoese retreated into the city and towards the harbour, while Constantine's men held their ground.</li><li><b>The Ottomans enter the city</b> The defenders were overwhelmed at several points along the wall, and the Janissaries pressed forward. The Venetians retreated to their ships, which left the Horn, and the Ottoman navy held the Golden Horn by midday.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Constantine XI Palaiologos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI Palaiologos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konstantinos_XI_Palaiologos_fresco_(less_edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ILoveHirasawaYui, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> had been an imperial capital since 330 and in eleven centuries had been besieged many times but taken only once, in 1204 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. The Nicaeans recaptured it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> in 1261 and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a>, which then fended off Latins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and Ottoman Turks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> killed almost half of the city's inhabitants between 1346 and 1349. By 1453 the city was a series of walled villages separated by fields, inside the fifth-century Theodosian walls, and by 1450 the empire had shrunk to a few square kilometres outside the city, the Princes' Islands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> succeeded his father in 1451 at the age of 19, and many European courts assumed that he would not challenge Christian hegemony in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. By early 1452 he was building a second fortress, Rumeli Hisari, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> from Anadolu Hisari, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> control of sea traffic on the strait. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a> turned to the West, but the union of the Eastern and Western churches that Pope Nicholas V wanted was bitterly opposed in Constantinople, and the court declared it valid only on 12 December 1452. The Pope had less influence over Western rulers than the Byzantines hoped, and those rulers were occupied by wars of their own. Some help came: Cardinal Isidore with 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and Giovanni Giustiniani from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> with 400 men, joined by 300 from Genoese Chios. A Venetian fleet was delayed until April and came too late, and seven Italian ships with about 700 men slipped away when Giustiniani arrived.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Constantinople&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Preparations, European support, Great Chain of the Golden Horn:</p><ul><li>Steven Runciman, The Fall of Constantinople, 1453 (Cambridge University Press, 1965)</li><li>Donald M. Nicol, The Last Centuries of Byzantium, 1261–1453 (Cambridge University Press, 1993)</li><li>David Nicolle, Constantinople 1453: The End of Byzantium (Campaign) (Osprey Publishing, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/janissaries"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janissaries.jpg" alt="Janissaries" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lambert_Wyts_-_Agha_of_the_Janissaries_and_a_B%C3%B6l%C3%BCk_of_the_Janissaries.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lambert Wyts, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The defenders numbered about 7,000, 2,000 of them foreigners. Constantine ordered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> at the end of March, and his chancellor George Sphrantzes recorded 4,773 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and just 200 foreigners, with volunteers from outside, Genoese, Venetians and others from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a>, who numbered hardly as many as three thousand: under 8,000 in all to hold twelve miles of wall. Fewer than 50,000 people were living in the city. The land walls were 5.5 km, with sea walls along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> (7 km) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> (7.5 km), and the defenders had 26 ships and a chain across the mouth of the Golden Horn. Constantine and his Greek troops held the Mesoteichion, where the river Lycus crossed the walls, and Giustiniani was first to the north and later beside him. The defenders' small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> proved ineffective, and the recoil tended to damage their own walls.</p><p>Recent studies and Ottoman archival data give some 50,000 to 80,000 Ottoman soldiers, including 5,000 to 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and 1,500 Serbian cavalry that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-brankovic">Đurađ Branković</a> was obliged to supply to the sultan, and 70 cannon. Western witnesses, who tended to exaggerate, give 160,000 (Barbaro), 200,000 (Tedaldi and Sphrantzes) and 300,000 (Isidore and Leonardo of Chios). Contemporary estimates of the Ottoman fleet run from 110 ships to 430, and a modern estimate is 110. Cannon are given as 12 to 62. The best known was the bombard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orban">Orban</a>, a Hungarian (some say German) founder, said to throw a 600-pound (270 kg) stone ball over a mile, though it took three hours to reload.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Constantinople&#8221; cites where it covers Strength:</p><ul><li>Steven Runciman, The Fall of Constantinople, 1453 (Cambridge University Press, 1965)</li><li>David Nicolle, Constantinople 1453: The End of Byzantium (Campaign) (Osprey Publishing, 2000)</li><li>Marios Philippides, The siege and the fall of Constantinople in 1453 (Ashgate, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii.jpg" alt="Mehmed II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bellini,_Gentile_-_Sultan_Mehmet_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gentile Bellini, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fired for weeks, but they were imprecise and slow, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> repaired most of the damage after each shot. The Ottoman fleet under Baltoghlu could not enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> because of the chain, although on 20 April four Christian ships fought their way in. On 22 April Mehmed dragged his ships overland into the Golden Horn, and on 28 April a fire-ship attack on them failed. Forty Italians who swam ashore were impaled on stakes by Mehmed's order, and in retaliation the defenders executed 260 Ottoman prisoners on the walls. Frontal assaults on the land walls were costly failures. From mid-May Ottoman sappers, many of them Serbian miners, dug tunnels, and the Byzantines, with counter-mines led by Johannes Grant, intercepted them on 16, 21, 23 and 25 May. On 21 May Mehmed offered to lift the siege and let the emperor and any others leave with their possessions; Constantine offered more tribute but refused to give up the city.</p><p>The assault began shortly after midnight on Tuesday 29 May. Christian troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> attacked first, then irregular azaps and Anatolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>, who breached the weaker Blachernae section but were pushed back, and last the elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>. Giustiniani was grievously wounded and his withdrawal caused panic. Constantine's men could not stop the Ottomans entering, and the defenders were overwhelmed at several points. The Venetians took to their ships and the Ottoman navy held the Golden Horn by midday.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Constantinople&#8221; cites where it covers Siege, Final assault:</p><ul><li>Steven Runciman, The Fall of Constantinople, 1453 (Cambridge University Press, 1965)</li><li>Donald M. Nicol, The Last Centuries of Byzantium, 1261–1453 (Cambridge University Press, 1993)</li><li>Marios Philippides, The siege and the fall of Constantinople in 1453 (Ashgate, 2011)</li><li>David Nicolle, Constantinople 1453: The End of Byzantium (Campaign) (Osprey Publishing, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia.jpg" alt="Hagia Sophia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Sophia_(228968325).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adli Wahid, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a> promised his soldiers three days of plunder, but sources say he rode in on the first day and called a halt, since little remained from 1204. Meanwhile troops who broke into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a> separated the crowd sheltering there by the price they might bring in the slave markets. Resisters were killed; Barbaro writes that the Turks "made a great slaughter of Christians" through the city. Leonard of Chios says sixty thousand Christians were captured; another count gives around 30,000 enslaved. David Nicolle says only about 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> died in the siege, and a Venetian Senate report says 50 noblemen and over 500 other Venetian civilians died. Philip Mansel writes of thousands of murders and rapes, and several chroniclers describe rape and enslavement.</p><p>Ottoman casualties are unknown but are believed by most historians to have been severe. When Mehmed saw the ruined city on 2 June he is reported to have wept. He then proclaimed that Christians who had avoided capture or had been ransomed could return home. He had Hagia Sophia turned into a mosque, appointed Gennadius Scholarius as patriarch, and made the city his capital. Constantine had died without an heir; his fate remains unknown and a severed head claimed to be his was nailed to a column. The sultan had the Catalan Pere Julià, his sons and the consul Joan de la Via, among others, beheaded. Doukas says Mehmed had Loukas Notaras executed after Notaras refused to give up his youngest son, a story Walter Andrew doubts.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Constantinople&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Steven Runciman, The Fall of Constantinople, 1453 (Cambridge University Press, 1965)</li><li>John Julius Norwich, Byzantium: The Decline and Fall (Alfred A. Knopf, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marios Philippides and Walter Hanak list 15 eyewitness accounts, 13 Christian and 2 Turkish, and 20 contemporary non-eyewitness accounts, 13 of them Italian. The eyewitnesses include Tursun Beg on the Ottoman side, George Sphrantzes, the only Greek eyewitness, whose account is laconic, Leonard of Chios, Nicolò Barbaro and Cardinal Isidore. Among the others are the Byzantine historians Doukas, Chalkokondyles and Critobulus, Ottoman chroniclers Ashikpashazade and Neshri, and the 17th-century traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a>, who collected local traditions. Makarios Melissourgos, who augmented Sphrantzes, is called not very reliable, and Nestor Iskander's chronicle often unreliable.</p><p>Several points are disputed. Western witnesses exaggerate the Ottoman army, so recent studies lean on Ottoman records. The report that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orban">Orban</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> collapsed after six weeks comes only from Leonard of Chios and Nestor Iskander. Barbaro says Constantine hanged himself, others that he died fighting in the streets, and his fate is unknown; later myths of his rescue have little evidence. Doukas's story of Notaras is doubted by Walter Andrew, who sees in it a story older than the conquest and a tendency to paint Muslims as morally inferior. The counts of the dead and enslaved differ between Nicolle, Leonard of Chios and the Venetian report. The claim that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">millet system</a> is now considered a myth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Pius II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pintoricchio_014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pinturicchio, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall marked the effective end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, a state dating from roughly 27 BCE, and for many modern historians the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Britannica says the Greek exodus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> marked the end of the Middle Ages and the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. It is called a turning point in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>, since walls that had protected the city for 800 years were overcome by Ottoman infantry with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed</a> called himself Kayser-i Rum, the Caesar of the Romans.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> the loss shocked many, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> argued for a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-cusa">Nicholas of Cusa</a> urged dialogue; no crusade came of Nicholas V's call. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a> fell in 1460 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1461. In the Islamic world the news was greeted with joy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> was decorated and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a> wrote to praise Mehmed. Legends grew, among them an eclipse on 22 May, a fog and a light about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, which may have been effects of a volcanic eruption, and the Marble Emperor, but they are little supported. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> have considered Tuesday unlucky since 29 May 1453. The city became officially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> only in 1930. In 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s president laid a wreath at Constantine's statue at Mystras.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Constantinople&#8221; cites where it covers Legends, Cultural impact:</p><ul><li>Steven Runciman, The Fall of Constantinople, 1453 (Cambridge University Press, 1965)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)<span>c. 1453</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>'s army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 29 May 1453 after a siege of 53 days, with defenders far fewer than the attackers. The city was plundered and many of its people killed or enslaved. It became the Ottoman capital, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> ended.</em></p>

